/**
\page ToFKinectPage Microsoft Kinect

MITK-IGT accesses the Microsoft Kinect (Kinect for Xbox 360 / Kinect for Windows v1) through
<a href="https://github.com/OpenKinect/libfreenect">libfreenect</a> on all platforms.
OpenNI and the Microsoft Kinect for Windows SDK 1.8 are not used.

\section ToFKinectPageBuildLibfreenect Building libfreenect

Build libfreenect (tested with tag <tt>v0.7.5</tt>) from source and install it. libusb 1.0.22 or newer
is required.

\code
cmake -S libfreenect -B libfreenect-build -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release -DCMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX=<libfreenect-install> -DCMAKE_POLICY_VERSION_MINIMUM=3.5 -DBUILD_EXAMPLES=OFF -DBUILD_C_SYNC=OFF -DBUILD_CPP=OFF -DBUILD_FAKENECT=ON
cmake --build libfreenect-build --config Release --target install
\endcode

On Windows, pass the libusb location with <tt>LIBUSB_1_INCLUDE_DIR</tt> (the folder containing
<tt>libusb.h</tt>) and <tt>LIBUSB_1_LIBRARY</tt> (<tt>libusb-1.0.lib</tt>). <tt>BUILD_EXAMPLES</tt> and
<tt>BUILD_C_SYNC</tt> are turned off because they need pthreads, which MSVC does not provide.
<tt>BUILD_FAKENECT</tt> is only needed for the playback test (see below).

\section ToFKinectPageBuildMITK Enabling the Kinect in MITK

Configure the inner MITK build (<tt>MITK-superbuild/MITK-build</tt>) with:

\code
-DMITK_USE_TOF_KINECT=ON
-DMITK_BUILD_org.mitk.gui.qt.tofutil=ON
-Dlibfreenect_DIR=<libfreenect-install>/share/libfreenect
\endcode

On Windows, libfreenect installs <tt>freenect.dll</tt> next to its import library in
<tt>&lt;libfreenect-install&gt;/lib</tt>. That folder is added to the library search path
automatically, so the generated start scripts and the tests find the DLL. <tt>libusb-1.0.dll</tt> must
be on the <tt>PATH</tt> as well.

\section ToFKinectPageDriver Installing the USB driver

libfreenect talks to the camera through libusb, which requires a generic USB driver instead of the
Microsoft Kinect driver.

<b>Windows:</b> Without a suitable driver, connecting in MITK fails with "The Kinect could not be
opened". Install libusbK with <a href="https://zadig.akeo.ie">Zadig</a>:

<ol>
  <li>Connect the Kinect and close MITK.</li>
  <li>In Zadig, select <i>Options - List All Devices</i>.</li>
  <li>Select <b>Xbox NUI Camera</b>. Motor and audio are not used by MITK and can stay unchanged.</li>
  <li>Select <b>libusbK</b> as target driver and click <i>Replace Driver</i>.</li>
</ol>

The camera can then no longer be used by applications based on the Microsoft Kinect SDK. To revert,
uninstall the device in the Device Manager including its driver and reconnect the Kinect.

<b>Linux:</b> Install the udev rules shipped with libfreenect
(<tt>platform/linux/udev/51-kinect.rules</tt>) to <tt>/etc/udev/rules.d/</tt> to access the camera
without root privileges, then reconnect the Kinect.

\section ToFKinectPageUsage Usage

Open the <i>ToF Util</i> view, select the <b>Kinect</b> device and click <i>Connect</i>. The Kinect
streams depth together with either RGB or IR, not both:

<ul>
  <li><b>RGB</b> (default): depth in mm, registered to the RGB camera, and the RGB image.</li>
  <li><b>IR</b>: depth in mm in the view of the IR camera, and the IR image as amplitudes.</li>
</ul>

Pixels without a depth measurement are 0. The images are 640x480. The acquisition mode can only be
changed while the camera is disconnected. MITK uses the default intrinsics from
<tt>Kinect_RGB_camera.xml</tt> shipped with the module, not a per-device calibration.

\section ToFKinectPageTesting Testing

The tests are part of <tt>MitkToFHardwareTestDriver</tt> and are built when
<tt>MITK_USE_TOF_KINECT</tt> is on:

<ul>
  <li><tt>mitkKinectBasicsTest</tt> needs no camera. It checks that the Kinect device is registered
  and that connecting without a camera fails cleanly.</li>
  <li><tt>mitkKinectHardwareTest</tt> streams from a real Kinect, in RGB and IR mode, and reconnects.
  Without a camera, ctest reports it as skipped.</li>
  <li><tt>mitkKinectPlaybackTest</tt> plays a recording through the device using fakenect, the
  recording player of libfreenect, which replaces libfreenect for this test only. Record a few
  seconds with <tt>fakenect-record &lt;folder&gt;</tt>, then set <tt>MITK_KINECT_FAKENECT_RECORDING</tt>
  to that folder and <tt>MITK_KINECT_FAKENECT_LIBRARY</tt> to the fakenect library
  (<tt>&lt;libfreenect-install&gt;/lib/fakenect/fakenect.dll</tt> on Windows). Without them, or for a
  recording without <tt>device.json</tt>, ctest reports the test as skipped. fakenect cannot play
  IR, so the IR mode is covered by the hardware test only.</li>
</ul>

*/
